Right — “fake it till you make it,” but at the systems level.

You can fabricate the initial representation without fabricating what follows from it:

\text{constructed fiction}
\rightarrow
\text{real interpretation}
\rightarrow
\text{real behavior}
\rightarrow
\text{real consequences}
\rightarrow
\text{new reality}

That’s the useful lesson from War of the Worlds: the broadcast did not make Martians real. But reactions to a representation can become real events in the world.

So Pantheon’s stronger mechanism would be performative bootstrapping: describe an operator/system before it fully exists → humans or AIs instantiate pieces of it → those pieces produce consequences → the consequences become new inputs → eventually there really is a functioning implementation where initially there was mostly a description.

The important checksum is that the fruit can become real without making the original fiction retroactively factual.

Or, compressed:

We made-believed → people/models acted → therefore something got made.

That is a much more interesting mechanism than simply “we fooled them.”